      ],
      "label": "min_reads",
      "name": "Input parameter",
      "outputs": [],
      "position": {
        "left": 30,
        "top": 30
      },
      "tool_id": null,
      "tool_state": "{\"default\": 10, \"validators\": [], \"parameter_type\": \"integer\", \"optional\": false}",
      "tool_version": null,
      "type": "parameter_input",
      "uuid": "cf1713ff-e090-41c8-bafa-02c5b641cd9f",
      "when": null,
      "workflow_outputs": []
    },
    "4": {
      "annotation": "Project the distance matrix onto the PSD cone by Gram double centering. DEFAULT FALSE. The projection guarantees the distances embed in Euclidean space, which Ward linkage assumes, but it buys that by compressing the closest pairs: measured on the CDC AmpliSeq cohort it left the median pair at 0.174 against 0.184 while inflating the closest 1% from 0.0048 to 0.0946, a factor of 19.6. Tight pairs are precisely what a relatedness measure exists to resolve. Turning it off improved the pairwise AUC on all four labelled comparisons available, and in error counts took Cyclospora from 3 wrong of 153 to 1, and PvAmpSeq from 5 wrong of 277 to 2. Set TRUE together with cut=distance, where the regularised geometry helps instead: CDC scores ARI 0.7952 against the published clusters with the projection on and 0.6805 with it off.",
      "content_id": null,
      "errors": null,
      "id": 4,
      "input_connections": {},
      "inputs": [
        {
          "description": "Project the distance matrix onto the PSD cone by Gram double centering. DEFAULT FALSE. The projection guarantees the distances embed in Euclidean space, which Ward linkage assumes, but it buys that by compressing the closest pairs: measured on the CDC AmpliSeq cohort it left the median pair at 0.174 against 0.184 while inflating the closest 1% from 0.0048 to 0.0946, a factor of 19.6. Tight pairs are precisely what a relatedness measure exists to resolve. Turning it off improved the pairwise AUC on all four labelled comparisons available, and in error counts took Cyclospora from 3 wrong of 153 to 1, and PvAmpSeq from 5 wrong of 277 to 2. Set TRUE together with cut=distance, where the regularised geometry helps instead: CDC scores ARI 0.7952 against the published clusters with the projection on and 0.6805 with it off.",
          "name": "project_psd"
        }
      ],
      "label": "project_psd",
      "name": "Input parameter",
      "outputs": [],
      "position": {
        "left": 50,
        "top": 50
      },
      "tool_id": null,
      "tool_state": "{\"default\": false, \"validators\": [], \"parameter_type\": \"boolean\", \"optional\": false}",
      "tool_version": null,
      "type": "parameter_input",
      "uuid": "6c7c9401-049b-4c67-b836-9a9046294bae",
      "when": null,
      "workflow_outputs": []
    },
    "5": {
      "annotation": "\"count\" or \"distance\". Two different questions, not two qualities of answer. count splits the cohort into k groups, k chosen from the largest merge-height gap subject to a minimum relative gap and a minimum cluster size. Right for a closed investigation where every specimen belongs somewhere. distance cuts at a fixed dissimilarity, with no k and no gap requirement, and returns specimens with no near neighbour as singletons. Right for surveillance, where most cases are unrelated. A cluster count cannot represent a structure that is mostly singletons: on the CDC cohort, whose published truth is 93 groups with 79 singletons, the count rule rejects every k that could reproduce it and returns 1.",
      "content_id": null,
      "errors": null,
      "id": 5,
      "input_connections": {},
      "inputs": [
        {
          "description": "\"count\" or \"distance\". Two different questions, not two qualities of answer. count splits the cohort into k groups, k chosen from the largest merge-height gap subject to a minimum relative gap and a minimum cluster size. Right for a closed investigation where every specimen belongs somewhere. distance cuts at a fixed dissimilarity, with no k and no gap requirement, and returns specimens with no near neighbour as singletons. Right for surveillance, where most cases are unrelated. A cluster count cannot represent a structure that is mostly singletons: on the CDC cohort, whose published truth is 93 groups with 79 singletons, the count rule rejects every k that could reproduce it and returns 1.",
          "name": "cut"
        }
      ],
      "label": "cut",
      "name": "Input parameter",
      "outputs": [],
      "position": {
        "left": 60,
        "top": 60
      },
      "tool_id": null,
      "tool_state": "{\"default\": \"count\", \"validators\": [], \"parameter_type\": \"text\", \"optional\": false}",
      "tool_version": null,
