    },
    "14": {
      "annotation": "Reduction: the sheet only means anything across specimens.\nmin_maf DEFAULTS TO 0, i.e. off. It was 0.05, justified by PyEuk weighting a column 1/sqrt(p(1-p)) -- minimised at p=0.5, divergent as p approaches 0, so rare alleles dominate. The pinned engine has not used that weighting since PyEuk 0.4.0: it defaults to heterozygosity 2p(1-p), under which rare columns hold 2.8% of the weight rather than 64.7%. The rationale was obsolete and the filter was doing measurable harm. At 0.05 on the Cyclospora cohort it deleted 33 of 48 columns and 19 of 28 windows, turned 2,443 called specimen-windows into empty locus blocks PyEuk reads as NOT CALLED, and left 82 specimen pairs sharing no locus at all -- their distance is then 1.0 by construction, not by measurement (max distance 1.3746 at 0.05, 0.7172 at 0). Scored against the vendor labels: ARI 0.9737 at 0.05 against 1.0000 at 0. On the Cryptosporidium titration it changes nothing -- same k=2, same 4/6 split, same pure-species controls on the correct sides, two extra columns.\nCaveat, measured not assumed: across five independently derived window sets on the same specimens, min_maf=0 gave 1.0000 on three, 0.9223 on one and 0.8001 on one, while 0.05 spanned a narrower 0.87-0.97. Zero raises the ceiling and widens the spread across windowings. Our deployed windowing is among the good ones, which is a property of this cohort rather than a general guarantee.\np is a fraction of SPECIMENS, not reads, so minor haplotypes within a mixture are untouched either way.",
      "content_id": "haplotype_window_sheet",
